From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: ntdll: a stack grows into read-write pages

When a thread touches its stack's guard page, Wine commits the page below as
the new guard -- keeping whatever protection the reservation had. Wine's own
stacks are reserved read-write, so that was invisible. The Cygwin/MSYS
runtime reserves its stacks PAGE_NOACCESS and commits the top pages
read-write itself, as Windows allows: there the grown page became committed
with no access, the next touch faulted again, and the process was killed by
SIGSEGV -- which Windows code sees as a clean exit with code 0. Git for
Windows' terminal (mintty) died that way in its first deep call
(EnumFontFamiliesExW, whose frame is 14 KB).

On Windows the kernel grows a stack into read-write pages whatever the
reservation's protection; now Wine does too, both for the new guard page and
when growing into the guaranteed space.

Gate: test/stackgrow-gate.sh -- a Cygwin-style stack, 200 KB of recursion in
a child; the child must live and the pages be read-write.
---
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -3998,15 +3998,21 @@
 
     set_page_vprot_bits( page, page_size, 0, VPROT_GUARD );
     mprotect_range( page, page_size, 0, 0 );
+    /* The pages the stack grows into are read-write, as Windows makes them,
+     * whatever protection the reservation had: a stack a program allocates
+     * itself (the Cygwin/MSYS runtime reserves PAGE_NOACCESS and commits the
+     * top pages read-write) would otherwise grow into committed pages with no
+     * access, and the next touch is a fatal access violation. */
     if (page >= stack_info->start + page_size + stack_info->guaranteed)
     {
-        set_page_vprot_bits( page - page_size, page_size, VPROT_COMMITTED | VPROT_GUARD, 0 );
+        set_page_vprot_bits( page - page_size, page_size,
+                             VPROT_COMMITTED | VPROT_GUARD | VPROT_READ | VPROT_WRITE, 0 );
         mprotect_range( page - page_size, page_size, 0, 0 );
     }
     else  /* inside guaranteed space -> overflow exception */
     {
         page = stack_info->start + page_size;
-        set_page_vprot_bits( page, stack_info->guaranteed, VPROT_COMMITTED, VPROT_GUARD );
+        set_page_vprot_bits( page, stack_info->guaranteed, VPROT_COMMITTED | VPROT_READ | VPROT_WRITE, VPROT_GUARD );
         mprotect_range( page, stack_info->guaranteed, 0, 0 );
         ret = STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW;
     }
